From: SamuriHL <samurihl@msn.com>
Subject: amdv: composite BL-only when the EL for a picture never arrives

A profile-7 FEL Blu-ray menu still goes black about a second after it is drawn,
keeps its audio, and comes back on any keypress. Measured three times on
Avatar: Fire and Ash. It predates this tree - it reproduces on release
20260906094636.

At the still the base layer emits its last picture before the enhancement layer
surfaces its partner, so dvel_vf_get() returns NULL and the EL block falls
through to the "not found el" arm. Two things then disagree:

  - video_func loses the frame's cur_dispbuf2, so dvel_swap_frame(NULL) sets
    need_disable_vd[1] and the EL layer is switched off - observed 138-141us
    after the error as "VID: VD1 off with async true".

  - el_flag is set unconditionally a few lines later from req.dv_enhance_exist,
    which vh265 forces to 1 for every frame of a frame-based dual-layer stream
    (vh265.c:9833). It is latched into dv_inst[].el_flag and pushed to the blob
    on every update, so core1a is left configured to composite an enhancement
    layer whose source layer is off. That composite is black.

Nothing recovers it because a still leaves toggle_mode at 0 (video_func.c:648),
so the frame is never re-toggled and the latched setting stands until new
content arrives - which is exactly what a keypress produces.

Everything reads healthy while the screen is black, which is what made this hard
to find: both decoders at matching frame counts with no drops, plane enabled,
disable_video 0, core1a on, dv_mode IPT_TUNNEL, HPD 1, avmute 0, and
/sys/class/video/frame_width reporting a live 3840 rather than "NA", so a good
frame really is committed to an enabled layer. The inconsistency is between
el_flag and a layer-enable bit and neither is visible in any of those. What does
show it is
    echo dv_el > /sys/class/amdolby_vision/debug
which prints new_m_dovi_setting.input[].el_flag - the value most recently handed
to the core. Taken while black it reads "inst0: el_flag: 1" with VID: VD1
already off.

Clear el_flag for that picture so the core composites base-layer-only, which is
what the already-disabled EL layer implies. This follows the existing idiom
immediately below, where el_flag is cleared when enable_mel/enable_fel refuse
the layer. It is per-frame: the next picture that pairs normally restores dual
layer, so ordinary playback sees at most a one-frame BL-only composite where it
currently sees a mismatch that the following frame papers over.

el_halfsize_flag must be cleared with it. It is initialised to 1 and cleared
only at the vf->width == el_vf->width test on the paired path, which needs a
non-NULL el_vf. Clearing el_flag alone therefore tells the core "no enhancement
layer" and "the enhancement layer is half size" at once, and the first build to
carry only the el_flag half rendered the menu with a green background instead of
black - visible, but a malformed composite.

Deliberately not setting amdv_el_disable here, unlike the enable_fel case. It is
reset only in dv_vf_reg_provider/dv_vf_light_reg_provider, so it is sticky for
the rest of the title and would disable the enhancement layer entirely after one
transient miss.

The deeper cause is that this SoC's wait path never holds the BL for its EL at
all: amdv_wait_metadata_v1 has a dual-layer block (is_dovi_dual_layer_frame ->
dvel_vf_peek -> return 1 to wait) and amdv_wait_metadata_v2, which multi_dv_mode
uses, has no equivalent - is_dovi_dual_layer_frame has exactly one call site
tree-wide. Porting that block is not done here: with FLAG_CHECK_ES_PTS clear
(the default) it degenerates to "is the EL queue empty", which cannot tell "the
EL arrives next vsync" from "the EL will never arrive", and the still is the
latter. Unbounded it would hold every vsync forever - which would look like a
fix, because the previous frame would stay on screen. That needs a vsync bound,
a VD1/instance-0 gate, and a MEL exclusion before it can be considered.

Not addressed, and unreachable as configured: on the pts-mismatch arm of the
same branch el_vf is taken by dvel_vf_get() and never returned with
dvel_vf_put(). FLAG_CHECK_ES_PTS is not in the default dolby_vision_flags, so a
non-NULL el_vf always takes the paired branch and that arm is dead code.

--- a/drivers/media/enhancement/amdolby_vision/amdv.c
+++ b/drivers/media/enhancement/amdolby_vision/amdv.c
@@ -9681,6 +9681,7 @@
 	bool dv_vsem = false;
 	bool el_flag = 0;
 	bool el_halfsize_flag = 1;
+	bool el_missing = false;
 	u32 w = 0xffff;
 	u32 h = 0xffff;
 	int meta_flag_bl = 1;
@@ -10052,6 +10053,7 @@
 					("bl(%p-%lld) not found el(%p-%lld)\n",
 					 vf, vf->pts_us64,
 					 el_vf, el_vf->pts_us64);
+				el_missing = true;
 			}
 		} else if (toggle_mode == 1) {
 			if (debug_dolby & 2)
@@ -10063,6 +10065,20 @@
 		if (req.dv_enhance_exist)
 			el_flag = 1;
 
+		/* No EL for this picture: the EL layer has already been switched
+		 * off, so leaving el_flag set asks core1a to composite an
+		 * enhancement layer that has no source, and that composite is
+		 * black. el_halfsize_flag has to go with it - it is cleared only
+		 * on the paired path, so a BL-only composite would otherwise
+		 * still be told the absent EL is half size, which renders green.
+		 * A still never re-toggles the frame, so either state would hold
+		 * until new content arrives.
+		 */
+		if (el_missing) {
+			el_flag = 0;
+			el_halfsize_flag = 0;
+		}
+
 		if (toggle_mode != 2) {
 			if (!drop_flag) {
 				dv_inst[dv_id].last_total_md_size = total_md_size;
